  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "We can use trend lines to predict values for data that follows a linear trend.\n\nConsider the scatter plot below that gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Let's predict the value of $y$ when $x = 5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14227574/assets/8ae392764c110d85f913353ddd932fad9d9c7fedde6275c2705cc7f3e92d3844.png)\n\nFirst, we sketch the trend line. If we don't know the trend line's equation, then we simply approximate it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14227574/assets/2e001c3f1e9dbdc2574913116a0fb73ac70396e97777c56b9d36b683d10d9814.png)\n\nTo make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $x = 5$ on the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14227574/assets/fb4f4ac52e4e2190b54cf9cf272e720729635c48387e6ac439ffcaf1282a2d7d.png)\n\n- Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14227574/assets/46a2e566d3810362756e6061e3120b7e7140a8a71591a6a1b90fd059b0505ae0.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the $y$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14227574/assets/8db3717a601f2e5b22c507385a04c60531d2ed057adc5372c5dddba174cf703f.png)\n\nTherefore, when $x = 5,$ we obtain $y \\approx 10.$",
